To regenerate every compatibility pickle answer file, from the repo root run:

    ./integration/compatibility/regenerate.sh

It works from the GENERATORS list in __init__.py, so it stays correct as
generators come and go. Today that list is:

    generate.py         -> aggregate-answers.pickle.gz
    generate_text.py    -> text-search-answers.pickle.gz
    generate_array.py   -> array-input-answers.pickle.gz

Or, to regenerate just one, cd to this directory and run pytest on it -- but
note that a run narrowed with -k, or one that fails part way, deliberately
declines to write its answer file, because a partial run would replace a
complete file with a short one.

Both forms require Docker. The generators spin up redis/redis-stack-server to
capture reference answers, in a container named Generate-search-NNNN on a port
docker assigns, so concurrent runs on one machine do not collide. A generator
that fails part way leaves its answer file untouched rather than replacing it
with a short one.

Each pickle stores a SHA256 of every .py file in this directory. The
compatibility integration test (integration/compatibility_test.py) verifies
that hash on load and fails if it does not match the current sources -- this
forces a regeneration whenever any source here is edited -- a generator,
data_sets.py, text_query_builder.py, or __init__.py itself.

generate_expr.py and expr-answers.pickle.gz are not in GENERATORS and so are
neither regenerated nor verified. The pickle also predates the hash wrapper
(it is a bare list of answers rather than a {sources_hash, answers} dict), so
nothing reads it today. Either register it or delete both.

To add a new generator: create generate_xxx.py (subclass BaseCompatibilityTest
with its own ANSWER_FILE_NAME) and add an entry to the GENERATORS list in
__init__.py. regenerate.sh and compatibility_test.py both read from that list.

To bypass the hash check (e.g. when generating a small pickle locally for
quick iteration), set:

    SKIP_COMPATIBILITY_HASH_CHECK=1
